><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20251113131509+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20251113131529+10'00'" name="d70cb28c-9d77-4e85-a29d-a550263c0212" page="6" coords="248.553833,727.872681,298.960693,727.872681,248.553833,716.842773,298.960693,716.842773,36.000000,716.874634,298.949097,716.874634,36.000000,705.844666,298.949097,705.844666,36.000000,705.876465,298.979218,705.876465,36.000000,694.846558,298.979218,694.846558,36.000000,694.878418,298.978638,694.878418,36.000000,683.848450,298.978638,683.848450,36.000000,683.880249,298.960876,683.880249,36.000000,672.850342,298.960876,672.850342,36.000000,672.882202,298.938843,672.882202,36.000000,661.852234,298.938843,661.852234,36.000000,661.884033,299.000366,661.884033,36.000000,650.854126,299.000366,650.854126,36.000000,650.885986,298.951111,650.885986,36.000000,639.856018,298.951111,639.856018,36.000000,639.887817,243.092651,639.887817,36.000000,628.857910,243.092651,628.857910" rect="35.655304,628.513184,299.345062,728.217407" subject="Comment on Text" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>However, the fusion of laboratory-based testing&#xD;within the daily training environment gives our results strong “realworld”&#xD;credibility, and the outcomes are highly relevant for elitelevel&#xD;triathletes. Although we have examined the acute impact of&#xD;this dietary strategy, the potential long-term effects of repeated</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
> </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>bouts of CHO restriction merit further investigation.</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
> </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Our findings&#xD;suggest that periodizing CHO availability to promote adaptation&#xD;throughout the training cycle had limited impact on the alterations&#xD;to the markers of athlete health, at least when occasional and&#xD;planned exposure to low CHO availability was undertaken with&#xD;expert nutrition and coaching input. Accordingly, when sessions&#xD;performed with low CHO availability are low intensity and short in&#xD;duration, there is likely only a minimal impact on exercise-induced&#xD;alterations in immune function and iron regulation.</span
></p
></body
